WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.068
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4427.

2
00:00:01.068 --> 00:00:02.136
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:02.492
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>Alright.

4
00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:04.629
So today is March twenty-seventh, 2015.

5
00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:10.326
And I’m Douglas Mungin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU Libraries.

6
00:00:10.326 --> 00:00:17.447
We were working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum, with Bill Shearman, to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

7
00:00:17.447 --> 00:00:25.992
And to kick that off, we are holding our second Mossville Oral History Day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:28.129
I’m here today with John Bernard.

9
00:00:28.129 --> 00:00:30.621
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:30.621 --> 00:00:35.250
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen to twenty minutes.

11
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:41.303
And we will likely be in touch with you later to set up a much longer interview.

12
00:00:41.303 --> 00:00:41.659
<v JOHN BERNARD>Right.

13
00:00:41.659 --> 00:00:43.795
<v MUNGIN>If you’re interested in continuing on.

14
00:00:43.795 --> 00:00:47.000
So could you please state your full name?

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.083
<v BERNARD>John Bernard.

16
00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:50.792
<v MUNGIN>And when were you born?

17
00:00:50.792 --> 00:00:54.583
<v BERNARD>I was born in 1931, in Lafayette.

18
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:56.750
<v MUNGIN>What part of Lafayette?

19
00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:57.833
<v BERNARD>Lafayette Parish.

20
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:02.167
It was in the country, on the farm.

21
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:05.417
<v MUNGIN>And what are your parents’ names?

22
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:06.500
<v BERNARD>[Arod?] Bernard.

23
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:11.917
My mama name was [Geramine Moiseus?], family name, you know.

24
00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:13.000
Yeah.

25
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.026
<v MUNGIN>And your grandparents’ names?

26
00:01:15.026 --> 00:01:15.532
<v BERNARD>Oh.

27
00:01:15.532 --> 00:01:18.065
My grandmother was [Ellie?] Mercier.

28
00:01:18.065 --> 00:01:18.571
Yeah.

29
00:01:18.571 --> 00:01:21.104
And Joe Mercier, my grandfather.

30
00:01:21.104 --> 00:01:25.662
<v MUNGIN>And is that your mom’s side or your dad’s?

31
00:01:25.662 --> 00:01:27.688
<v BERNARD>On my mama’s side.

32
00:01:27.688 --> 00:01:30.221
<v MUNGIN>What about your dad’s side?

33
00:01:30.221 --> 00:01:36.805
<v BERNARD>Oh, no, I don't know the history of that from my daddy’s side.

34
00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:39.844
<v MUNGIN>And is that a French name?

35
00:01:39.844 --> 00:01:40.857
Is that—

36
00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:41.364
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

37
00:01:41.364 --> 00:01:42.377
All French.

38
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:42.883
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

39
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:44.909
And were they French?

40
00:01:44.909 --> 00:01:47.948
<v BERNARD>They were from around Lafayette Parish.

41
00:01:47.948 --> 00:01:52.000
<v MUNGIN>What is your earliest memory of Mossville?

42
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.160
<v BERNARD>Oh, I forgot what year that I came down here.

43
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.240
It was way back when.

44
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.568
Old Spanish Trail was just a one-way highway.

45
00:02:01.568 --> 00:02:08.224
When I meet another car, one of them had to get off. [Laughs] Way back then.

46
00:02:08.224 --> 00:02:10.720
I’ve been here over sixty-some years.

47
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.632
And I forgot what year it was.

48
00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:19.456
But anyway, I raised all my kid right here, nine kids. [Unclear] nine kids,

49
00:02:19.456 --> 00:02:24.864
me and my wife, they all live, put them through school and everything.

50
00:02:24.864 --> 00:02:26.944
They all doing real good.

51
00:02:26.944 --> 00:02:31.104
<v MUNGIN>So what did Mossville look like when you came here?

52
00:02:31.104 --> 00:02:33.184
So were you a teenager?

53
00:02:33.184 --> 00:02:35.264
<v BERNARD>There were very few houses.

54
00:02:35.264 --> 00:02:42.336
This used to be, the schoolhouse used to be right in the front of my house here.

55
00:02:42.336 --> 00:02:45.248
The schoolhouse, that was a two-story building.

56
00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:46.080
One teacher.

57
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:46.912
One teacher.

58
00:02:46.912 --> 00:02:56.480
And it did have like a water trough, the kids go wash their hands and face and things right there in the yard.

59
00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.312
One teacher.

60
00:02:57.312 --> 00:03:00.224
And the teacher name was Ms. Lemelle.

61
00:03:00.224 --> 00:03:02.720
I don't know her real name.

62
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:03.552
Ms. Lemelle.

63
00:03:03.552 --> 00:03:07.712
And I bought this place here just passing through here.

64
00:03:07.712 --> 00:03:09.376
I bought two lot.

65
00:03:09.376 --> 00:03:12.704
And after that I was living in Houston.

66
00:03:12.704 --> 00:03:15.200
I was just passing through here.

67
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.696
I got a job at Conoco.

68
00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:20.608
And I worked there until I retired.

69
00:03:20.608 --> 00:03:23.104
<v MUNGIN>What was your job at Conoco?

70
00:03:23.104 --> 00:03:23.520
<v BERNARD>Huh?

71
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.016
<v MUNGIN>What was your job at Conoco?

72
00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:26.848
<v BERNARD>[unclear] Yeah.

73
00:03:26.848 --> 00:03:27.264
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

74
00:03:27.264 --> 00:03:33.088
So you were just driving through here and just decided to buy a house?

75
00:03:33.088 --> 00:03:36.000
<v BERNARD>No. I got a job here.

76
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:36.380
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

77
00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.661
<v BERNARD>I got a job out here.

78
00:03:38.661 --> 00:03:42.463
My brother-in-law was working there, he said they were hiring.

79
00:03:42.463 --> 00:03:44.364
I went to see them.

80
00:03:44.364 --> 00:03:47.025
They hired me right off the bat.

81
00:03:47.025 --> 00:03:51.587
And I worked at it five years in the Conoco oil refinery.

82
00:03:51.587 --> 00:03:52.727
Really love it.

83
00:03:52.727 --> 00:03:56.909
<v MUNGIN>So what did Mossville look like when you first moved here?

84
00:03:56.909 --> 00:04:02.612
<v BERNARD>It was so small, man, you could have count the house they had out here!

85
00:04:02.612 --> 00:04:05.273
They had cattle running all around here!

86
00:04:05.273 --> 00:04:07.934
They had, them woods was all clean.

87
00:04:07.934 --> 00:04:09.835
You could walk from one

88
00:04:09.835 --> 00:04:11.736
<c.p>end to the other, clean.

89
00:04:11.736 --> 00:04:15.537
Them cows was loose and they keep that grass down.

90
00:04:15.537 --> 00:04:20.860
And after that they did, [unclear] those cows and just everything started growing up.

91
00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.000
Oh, yeah.

92
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.667
<v MUNGIN>And so when did it start to build up?

93
00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:28.926
<v BERNARD>Oh, after that, every year people started building.

94
00:04:28.926 --> 00:04:33.407
People started migrating here, working in the plants and what not.

95
00:04:33.407 --> 00:04:36.667
And that’s when all of this started growing.

96
00:04:36.667 --> 00:04:39.519
<v MUNGIN>And what year did you move here?

97
00:04:39.519 --> 00:04:41.556
<v BERNARD>Oh, I forgot what year.

98
00:04:41.556 --> 00:04:44.000
My memory’s kind of weak. [laughs]

99
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.481
<v MUNGIN>So where did you, so you lived in Lafayette growing up?

100
00:04:48.481 --> 00:04:50.519
<v BERNARD>It was in the country.

101
00:04:50.519 --> 00:04:55.000
It was on the farm, out there in the country.

102
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:59.908
<v MUNGIN>And how was life like in Lafayette at that time?

103
00:04:59.908 --> 00:05:01.871
<v BERNARD>Oh, it was great.

104
00:05:01.871 --> 00:05:04.325
It was on the farm.

105
00:05:04.325 --> 00:05:06.779
We was a big family.

106
00:05:06.779 --> 00:05:10.706
We were twelve of us on the farm.

107
00:05:10.706 --> 00:05:14.632
Raised cotton, corn, sweet potatoes, everything right there.

108
00:05:14.632 --> 00:05:19.049
We didn’t go to the store and buy anything.

109
00:05:19.049 --> 00:05:21.503
We raised everything we wanted.

110
00:05:21.503 --> 00:05:22.485
Chicken, hog.

111
00:05:22.485 --> 00:05:22.975
Geese.

112
00:05:22.975 --> 00:05:28.865
Ducks. [laughs] You didn’t have to go to the store for nothing.

113
00:05:28.865 --> 00:05:36.227
<v MUNGIN>So when you moved to Mossville, how was the house, the property that you bought?

114
00:05:36.227 --> 00:05:39.663
<v BERNARD>It didn’t have no house at all.

115
00:05:39.663 --> 00:05:42.117
It was just wood area.

116
00:05:42.117 --> 00:05:44.571
And I clean it up.

117
00:05:44.571 --> 00:05:50.951
And finally after a couple of years, that’s when I decide to build.

118
00:05:50.951 --> 00:05:56.350
I started building and I built a big house right there.

119
00:05:56.350 --> 00:06:01.748
It’s a three-bedroom, four-bedroom, big living room, big kitchen, dining room.

120
00:06:01.748 --> 00:06:05.184
<v MUNGIN>And how many kids do you have?

121
00:06:05.184 --> 00:06:05.675
<v BERNARD>Nine.

122
00:06:05.675 --> 00:06:10.583
Six girls and three boys. [break] They’re doing real good.

123
00:06:10.583 --> 00:06:15.000
Never did give me no trouble for nothing.

124
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.956
<v MUNGIN>And they were all raised in Mossville.

125
00:06:17.956 --> 00:06:28.511
<v BERNARD>All raise in our, bunch of kid in the neighborhood, they thought my house was a recreation center. [laughs] They all come at my house.

126
00:06:28.511 --> 00:06:31.044
I bought me a big van.

127
00:06:31.044 --> 00:06:34.844
And on the weekend, we’d go out of town.

128
00:06:34.844 --> 00:06:36.956
Them kids, “Can we go?

129
00:06:36.956 --> 00:06:37.800
Can we?”

130
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.333
They’d bring so many of them.

131
00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:43.289
I’d say, “Well, I got a load.

132
00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:45.822
I can’t bring no more.” [laughs].

133
00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:49.200
Boy, they loved to go in the country.

134
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.044
Have fun.

135
00:06:50.044 --> 00:06:54.689
That’s one of them right them right there on the porch.

136
00:06:54.689 --> 00:06:57.644
Edward Julia used to come out there.

137
00:06:57.644 --> 00:06:59.333
Her and her sister.

138
00:06:59.333 --> 00:06:59.756
Yeah.

139
00:06:59.756 --> 00:07:02.289
I had a twin brother, too.

140
00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:06.089
He was crazy about my twin brother. [laughs] Yeah.

141
00:07:06.089 --> 00:07:08.200
But everything worked really nice.

142
00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:08.622
Yeah.

143
00:07:08.622 --> 00:07:14.956
<v MUNGIN>And so were there a lot of kids just kind of running through the area?

144
00:07:14.956 --> 00:07:17.489
<v BERNARD>A lot of kids come, yeah.

145
00:07:17.489 --> 00:07:19.178
Yeah [unclear] come up.

146
00:07:19.178 --> 00:07:25.511
They used to have a bunch of people [unclear] then that Conoco bought them out.

147
00:07:25.511 --> 00:07:31.000
And after Conoco bought them out, here come Sasol bought Mossville here.

148
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.545
<v MUNGIN>And how long ago was that?

149
00:07:33.545 --> 00:07:33.970
<v BERNARD>Huh?

150
00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.212
<v MUNGIN>How long ago was it when Conoco bought people out?

151
00:07:38.212 --> 00:07:44.152
<v BERNARD>It was about ten years, about ten years ago, Conoco bought all Bel Air.

152
00:07:44.152 --> 00:07:47.970
<v MUNGIN>And so your house was a pretty big house.

153
00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:52.636
So did you guys have like a garden or livestock there?

154
00:07:52.636 --> 00:07:53.061
<v BERNARD>What?

155
00:07:53.061 --> 00:07:54.333
<v MUNGIN>In your house?

156
00:07:54.333 --> 00:07:55.606
At your house?

157
00:07:55.606 --> 00:07:56.030
<v BERNARD>No.

158
00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:56.879
<v MUNGIN>No garden?

159
00:07:56.879 --> 00:07:57.727
<v BERNARD>Uh uh.

160
00:07:57.727 --> 00:07:58.576
<v MUNGIN>No livestock?

161
00:07:58.576 --> 00:07:59.000
<v BERNARD>No.

162
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.545
<v MUNGIN>So where did you guys shop?

163
00:08:01.545 --> 00:08:01.970
<v BERNARD>Huh?

164
00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:05.364
<v MUNGIN>Where did you guys get your food from?

165
00:08:05.364 --> 00:08:09.182
<v BERNARD>Oh, the Market Basket in West Lake or Sulphur.

166
00:08:09.182 --> 00:08:13.000
<v MUNGIN>And what school did your kids go to?

167
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:13.925
<v BERNARD>Mossville School.

168
00:08:13.925 --> 00:08:16.698
Mossville School and West Lake High.

169
00:08:16.698 --> 00:08:17.161
Yeah.

170
00:08:17.161 --> 00:08:21.784
I’ve got one of my daughters, she’s an x-ray technician.

171
00:08:21.784 --> 00:08:30.106
Now she live, she retired from Saint Pat, worked twenty-some odd years, 25 years, at that Saint Pat.

172
00:08:30.106 --> 00:08:35.191
And she retired from that and went to work in Houston.

173
00:08:35.191 --> 00:08:38.427
Now she running three hospitals in Houston.

174
00:08:38.427 --> 00:08:42.126
Cancer hospital and three hospitals, [unclear] other name.

175
00:08:42.126 --> 00:08:49.985
<v MUNGIN>So when you first moved to Mossville, who were some of the community leaders in this area?

176
00:08:49.985 --> 00:08:54.608
I know you were saying it was kind of small.

177
00:08:54.608 --> 00:08:59.231
<v BERNARD>They had one old man, his name was Josh Rigmaiden.

178
00:08:59.231 --> 00:09:02.930
He was like a little mayor around here.

179
00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.704
Everybody go to him for everything.

180
00:09:05.704 --> 00:09:12.638
And he used to have a little store right out in front of his house.

181
00:09:12.638 --> 00:09:17.261
Everybody used to go to him to buy different things.

182
00:09:17.261 --> 00:09:23.734
Very few cars, you see car here in Mossville when I moved down here.

183
00:09:23.734 --> 00:09:28.819
Yeah, they didn’t have much cars, nobody didn’t have no cars.

184
00:09:28.819 --> 00:09:29.281
Yup.

185
00:09:29.281 --> 00:09:33.905
They was logging some trees, cutting some trees, [poke?] wood.

186
00:09:33.905 --> 00:09:35.754
They were [poke?] wood.

187
00:09:35.754 --> 00:09:40.377
Right along that [unclear] they was loading up [poke?] wood.

188
00:09:40.377 --> 00:09:45.000
That would have been a long time ago. [laughs]

189
00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:55.055
<v MUNGIN>And so as Mossville continued to grow, who were some of the folks that kind of became leaders of this community?

190
00:09:55.055 --> 00:09:58.885
<v BERNARD>They, a few of them came after that.

191
00:09:58.885 --> 00:10:02.236
After he died, there was some Moss.

192
00:10:02.236 --> 00:10:03.194
Dallas Moss.

193
00:10:03.194 --> 00:10:07.503
And see if I can think of his name.

194
00:10:07.503 --> 00:10:09.897
Ellis Moss and Jim Rigmaiden.

195
00:10:09.897 --> 00:10:13.248
Is that his name was Jack Rigmaiden.

196
00:10:13.248 --> 00:10:19.473
They was all born and raised around here, and they native from here.

197
00:10:19.473 --> 00:10:20.909
From way back.

198
00:10:20.909 --> 00:10:24.739
They was all staying right there in Mossville.

199
00:10:24.739 --> 00:10:29.048
They grew up, some of them left and everything.

200
00:10:29.048 --> 00:10:30.485
And people die.

201
00:10:30.485 --> 00:10:32.879
Very few of them left.

202
00:10:32.879 --> 00:10:37.667
I’m probably the oldest one left here in Mossville now.

203
00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:40.061
I’ll be 55 next month.

204
00:10:40.061 --> 00:10:42.455
So thank God for that.

205
00:10:42.455 --> 00:10:43.412
Getting around.

206
00:10:43.412 --> 00:10:50.594
<v MUNGIN>So a lot of the people that we’ve been interviewing have been born in Mossville.

207
00:10:50.594 --> 00:10:54.903
You’re kind of one of the few folks that—

208
00:10:54.903 --> 00:10:56.818
<v BERNARD>I migrate over here.

209
00:10:56.818 --> 00:11:03.042
<v MUNGIN>Who has actually migrated here and has been here longer than they’ve been.

210
00:11:03.042 --> 00:11:04.000
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

211
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:10.816
<v MUNGIN>So could you talk a little bit about how you viewed this community when you first got here,

212
00:11:10.816 --> 00:11:17.252
compared to like, you know, being a person who’s been in the community for a long time.

213
00:11:17.252 --> 00:11:22.932
<v BERNARD>Well, I come through here, [like I said?] good job and work at the plant.

214
00:11:22.932 --> 00:11:29.748
And at [unclear] time that wasn’t playing that much, but I see there was a steady job, so.

215
00:11:29.748 --> 00:11:32.777
And I’ve been working then until I retire.

216
00:11:32.777 --> 00:11:34.291
And keep on going.

217
00:11:34.291 --> 00:11:38.835
When I first went there, I wasn’t making but $1.75 an hour.

218
00:11:38.835 --> 00:11:43.757
And when I left there I was making 24, 27 dollars an hour.

219
00:11:43.757 --> 00:11:45.650
I was getting top pay.

220
00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:50.573
More than some of them guys were working operation, I had the seniority.

221
00:11:50.573 --> 00:11:58.146
So they come along and made an offer all those had enough time to retire, go ahead on and retire.

222
00:11:58.146 --> 00:12:01.553
I was only 55 years old when I retired.

223
00:12:01.553 --> 00:12:04.204
But I had enough time to retire.

224
00:12:04.204 --> 00:12:07.233
I said well, I might as well go.

225
00:12:07.233 --> 00:12:09.505
So far, I’ve stayed here. [laughs]

226
00:12:09.505 --> 00:12:17.456
<v MUNGIN>So could you talk a little bit about some of the places that have kind of come and gone in Mossville--

227
00:12:17.456 --> 00:12:17.835
<v BERNARD>Yeah.

228
00:12:17.835 --> 00:12:22.000
<v MUNGIN>--since the time that you first got here till now.

229
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.318
<v BERNARD>Oh, yeah, my brother-in-law would stay right there on Prater Street.

230
00:12:27.318 --> 00:12:33.603
He’d be at a big club, he’d run that for twenty-some odd years.

231
00:12:33.603 --> 00:12:36.021
My brother-in-law and my sister.

232
00:12:36.021 --> 00:12:39.888
They run that club for a long time.

233
00:12:39.888 --> 00:12:42.306
And now he got Alzheimer’s.

234
00:12:42.306 --> 00:12:45.207
He don’t know nothing no more.

235
00:12:45.207 --> 00:12:46.657
My sister passed.

236
00:12:46.657 --> 00:12:51.975
Yeah, he was in business with vacuum truck over at Conoco.

237
00:12:51.975 --> 00:12:54.393
He had some vacuum truck.

238
00:12:54.393 --> 00:12:58.744
Him and, his brother had some dump truck, too.

239
00:12:58.744 --> 00:13:00.678
Had some vacuum truck.

240
00:13:00.678 --> 00:13:03.579
Hydro blasting, working in the plant.

241
00:13:03.579 --> 00:13:05.029
We made it.

242
00:13:05.029 --> 00:13:09.864
<v MUNGIN>Were there other buildings like that were important in this—

243
00:13:09.864 --> 00:13:13.248
<v BERNARD>Not Moss – no. Mossville was just small.

244
00:13:13.248 --> 00:13:17.599
And they opened a subdivision out there at Queensboro.

245
00:13:17.599 --> 00:13:20.500
And it still right there now.

246
00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:25.818
And they buying the people out, people just living out here.

247
00:13:25.818 --> 00:13:29.686
I wish I knew what I knew today.

248
00:13:29.686 --> 00:13:32.587
I could have bought all Mossville.

249
00:13:32.587 --> 00:13:40.806
When I first come I see in Mossville, the property was selling at 100 dollars an acre.

250
00:13:40.806 --> 00:13:43.223
A hundred dollars an acre.

251
00:13:43.223 --> 00:13:51.926
But everybody didn’t have that hundred dollars. [laughs] I bought two lot, it was a few years after.

252
00:13:51.926 --> 00:13:58.211
My brother-in-law, when he bought there, well he had, guess what he paid.

253
00:13:58.211 --> 00:14:00.145
He bought two lots.

254
00:14:00.145 --> 00:14:01.595
Two hundred dollars.

255
00:14:01.595 --> 00:14:04.012
And he was cutting [pokewood?].

256
00:14:04.012 --> 00:14:04.496
Yeah.

257
00:14:04.496 --> 00:14:06.430
With a crosscut saw.

258
00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:11.748
You know one you—yeah, that’s what he was cutting wood with.

259
00:14:11.748 --> 00:14:13.682
Him and his brother.

260
00:14:13.682 --> 00:14:19.000
It’s just one of them things, just normal, normal life.

261
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:23.719
<v MUNGIN>And how much are those lots going for now?

262
00:14:23.719 --> 00:14:24.243
<v BERNARD>Huh?

263
00:14:24.243 --> 00:14:28.438
<v MUNGIN>How much are the lots going for now?

264
00:14:28.438 --> 00:14:29.486
<v BERNARD>Oh, shoot.

265
00:14:29.486 --> 00:14:33.156
It’s up in the thousands and thousands.

266
00:14:33.156 --> 00:14:34.205
Oh, yeah.

267
00:14:34.205 --> 00:14:39.448
One lot pay right close to ten thousand dollars for.

268
00:14:39.448 --> 00:14:42.594
Like Conoco made us an offer.

269
00:14:42.594 --> 00:14:48.361
Then [unclear] made me offers here, I had four rent houses.

270
00:14:48.361 --> 00:14:52.031
And I sold it with my house.

271
00:14:52.031 --> 00:15:03.566
Looked like to me I could have had more than that, because that’s five house all together I’m selling as a package.

272
00:15:03.566 --> 00:15:08.809
And I had a little over an acre of land.

273
00:15:08.809 --> 00:15:14.052
I’ve been having rent house all over 30, 40 years.

274
00:15:14.052 --> 00:15:15.625
And discontinued now.

275
00:15:15.625 --> 00:15:20.344
I ain’t got no more income coming in now.

276
00:15:20.344 --> 00:15:27.160
I wish, I’m getting to where I want to give it up anyway.

277
00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:35.549
So I retired from that. (laughs) Yeah, but I love it out there in Moss Bluff.

278
00:15:35.549 --> 00:15:39.219
I live out there now, in Gateway.

279
00:15:39.219 --> 00:15:56.521
<v MUNGIN>So were there any important families in this area that kind of like really helped build up Mossville from where it was as a tiny town to kind of where it was, eventually?

280
00:15:56.521 --> 00:15:57.569
<v BERNARD>Oh, yeah.

281
00:15:57.569 --> 00:16:04.385
But see the people, when they move like that, they build them houses.

282
00:16:04.385 --> 00:16:11.726
But all [some of them?] been out here, they didn’t improve nothing on it.

283
00:16:11.726 --> 00:16:14.872
No. And they had the chance.

284
00:16:14.872 --> 00:16:21.688
They was born and raised right here, but there wasn’t progress on them.

285
00:16:21.688 --> 00:16:25.358
And then the people started moving in.

286
00:16:25.358 --> 00:16:29.552
That’s when they started building houses and growing.

287
00:16:29.552 --> 00:16:32.174
But I’m [unclear] in Mossville.

288
00:16:32.174 --> 00:16:38.465
And been at the recreation [unclear] all the children come along here.

289
00:16:38.465 --> 00:16:43.708
It was really have nice time, people enjoyed us there.

290
00:16:43.708 --> 00:16:47.378
Had a lot of people out here.

291
00:16:47.378 --> 00:16:50.000
But they moving out.

292
00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:53.617
<v MUNGIN>So I got a couple more questions for you.

293
00:16:53.617 --> 00:16:56.028
What does home mean to you?

294
00:16:56.028 --> 00:16:58.037
<v BERNARD>It mean a whole lot.

295
00:16:58.037 --> 00:17:03.262
When you raised your family there, stayed there over 60- some odd years.

296
00:17:03.262 --> 00:17:08.486
That mean a whole lot. [unclear] house, say I’m glad I’m moving out.

297
00:17:08.486 --> 00:17:11.299
I raised my children here and everything.

298
00:17:11.299 --> 00:17:16.121
Well, I had a chance to sell it, and I sold it.

299
00:17:16.121 --> 00:17:21.748
And of course nobody else wouldn’t give me that, what I got for it.

300
00:17:21.748 --> 00:17:25.364
Anyhow, it was put on the market to sell.

301
00:17:25.364 --> 00:17:26.570
So I’m satisfied.

302
00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:28.981
<v MUNGIN>What does community mean to you?

303
00:17:28.981 --> 00:17:30.991
<v BERNARD>It mean a whole lot.

304
00:17:30.991 --> 00:17:32.196
But nice history.

305
00:17:32.196 --> 00:17:35.813
Like my wife, [Camille daughter?], did, look at that.

306
00:17:35.813 --> 00:17:39.832
She [cried?] save up for whatever house, it’s gone now.

307
00:17:39.832 --> 00:17:47.467
I built the house myself, from the ground on up. [unclear] It took me three months to build it,

308
00:17:47.467 --> 00:17:50.682
but we built it myself, and a carpenter.

309
00:17:50.682 --> 00:17:51.084
Yeah.

310
00:17:51.084 --> 00:17:53.897
We didn’t have no contractor or nothing.

311
00:17:53.897 --> 00:17:57.916
<v MUNGIN>And so how does your wife feel about the move?

312
00:17:57.916 --> 00:17:59.523
<v BERNARD>Well, she like it.

313
00:17:59.523 --> 00:18:04.346
She wanted to get out of here, the way things going now.

314
00:18:04.346 --> 00:18:10.374
And all that traffic and things like that, she might as well move on out.

315
00:18:10.374 --> 00:18:14.393
And you can, we found another nice place over there.

316
00:18:14.393 --> 00:18:16.000
We like it.

317
00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:19.317
<v MUNGIN>How has the community in Mossville changed?

318
00:18:19.317 --> 00:18:19.791
<v BERNARD>How—

319
00:18:19.791 --> 00:18:21.213
<v MUNGIN>Has it changed?

320
00:18:21.213 --> 00:18:23.108
And if so, how?

321
00:18:23.108 --> 00:18:25.004
<v BERNARD>Because everybody moving out.

322
00:18:25.004 --> 00:18:33.060
It’s not people just, they [unclear] everybody moving out that some of them don’t want to move.

323
00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:36.377
So that’s the history here, you know.

324
00:18:36.377 --> 00:18:43.011
But to me, I rather move than stay in the, next to the plant.

325
00:18:43.011 --> 00:18:47.276
I know what it is the plant can do.

326
00:18:47.276 --> 00:18:52.489
Blow it off man, blow your head off. (laughs) Hope not.

327
00:18:52.489 --> 00:18:53.437
<v MUNGIN>Last question.

328
00:18:53.437 --> 00:18:59.123
What’s the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville?

329
00:18:59.123 --> 00:19:01.493
<v BERNARD>Mossville was a nice community.

330
00:19:01.493 --> 00:19:03.862
People sticking together and everything.

331
00:19:03.862 --> 00:19:04.810
Real great.

332
00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.179
Everybody get along real good.

333
00:19:07.179 --> 00:19:10.970
And it was a nice community out here.

334
00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:15.235
And they had a lot of people out there.

335
00:19:15.235 --> 00:19:22.343
People used to come play ball right in front here. [unclear] had a great town.

336
00:19:22.343 --> 00:19:27.082
Yeah, every time they have something, they be right here.

337
00:19:27.082 --> 00:19:28.504
Headed right here.

338
00:19:28.504 --> 00:19:29.451
Really nice.

339
00:19:29.451 --> 00:19:33.243
In the summertime, they had that swimming pool.

340
00:19:33.243 --> 00:19:42.246
They had so much people out there having fun and everything with not a, and nobody go in there.

341
00:19:42.246 --> 00:19:46.985
If they got it open, but ain’t no more kids.

342
00:19:46.985 --> 00:19:47.933
<v MUNGIN>All right.

343
00:19:47.933 --> 00:19:50.302
So our time is up.

344
00:19:50.302 --> 00:19:56.937
On behalf of the center and on the museum, we appreciate your contribution today.

345
00:19:56.937 --> 00:19:57.884
<v BERNARD>Thank you.

346
00:19:57.884 --> 00:19:58.832
Thank you.

347
00:19:58.832 --> 00:20:08.784
<v MUNGIN>Do you have anything that you want to add in closing that I haven’t asked or you haven’t said so far?

348
00:20:08.784 --> 00:20:10.205
<v BERNARD>No, not really.

349
00:20:10.205 --> 00:20:17.313
I’m going to have my daughter to come meet you all whenever you come back.

350
00:20:17.313 --> 00:20:23.000
She can tell you a whole lot of things, my children.

351
00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:23.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4427.

352
00:20:23.429 --> 00:20:23.857
End Session I.]
